some 2 million women across the nation  forced to leave the workforce when  schools close during the pandemic and  just because businesses are back open  and schools resume doesn't necessarily  mean things will return to normal  well-being expert lorna borenstein has  the following message for managers heads  of companies that all the adjustments  your team's made during that time won't  magically disappear she says there are a  number of things ceos can do to make the  return on their employees easier  maybe they need no meetings before 8 30  in the morning maybe they need no  meeting zones from 5 to 7 p.m  maybe they need some flexibility i  remember when i used to work retail very  early on in my career i wasn't even  allowed to swap my hours with somebody  else only a manager could do flexible  time swaps what why  give people flexibility to help them  adjust so number one understand just  because you want them back and you know  back in the store back in the office  doesn't mean that they've got boom  everything back to the way it was  takes a little time bornstein also says  it's important to what she calls parent  loudly in the workplace she remembers  having to sneak out of her job to say  goodnight to her kids she says it's  important to show others you have a life  that you're juggling and it's okay to  talk about things that are real if  you're dealing with a crisis it's okay  to talk to your manager about it and she  says managers in turn should want to  create a safe environment for you to ask  for help
 

